<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Oiniwar dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1325 CE – 1526 CE (approx.) · capital Sugauna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Maithil Brahmin dynasty that ruled Mithila for two centuries, famous as patrons of the poet Vidyapati.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karnat-dynasty">Karnat dynasty</a> in 1324, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughluq</a> rulers granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kameshwar-thakur">Kameshwar Thakur</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> of the Oinwar family, to govern on their behalf. His descendants ruled the region north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> as tributary kings, with seats at Sugauna and elsewhere.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Oiniwar kings were scholars as well as rulers and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> learning, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyaya">Nyaya</a> logic. Their most famous protege was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidyapati">Vidyapati</a>, whose love songs in Maithili are classics of eastern Indian literature; he served kings such as Shivasimha. The kings balanced between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jaunpur-sultanate">Jaunpur Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, the dynasty faded in the early 16th century, traditionally ending in 1526. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> later passed under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, which recognised the Khandavala family, the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raj-darbhanga">Raj Darbhanga</a>, as its leading landholders.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/oiniwar-dynasty.jpg" alt="Oiniwar dynasty" width="320" height="143"><figcaption>Image: Archaeological Survey of India · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silve_coin_of_Bhairavasimha.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1325 CE – 1526 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sugauna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kameshwar Thakur, Shivasimha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Maithili, Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oinwaras of Mithila, Sugauna dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1426&amp;focus=oiniwar-dynasty" aria-label="Oiniwar dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6955 1208 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Oiniwar dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7275 1464L7241 1447M7275 1464L7018 1385M7275 1464L7187 1487"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7241 1447h0M7018 1385h0M7187 1487h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7275 1464h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7275 1464h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sugauna. <a href="/atlas?year=1426&amp;focus=oiniwar-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
